+"""Debug-only allocator telemetry for E2, r4 BOUNDARY-ONLY. Off unless VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS=<ctl poll seconds> and
+VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS_DIR=<dir>.
+
+WHY THIS IS NOT A SAMPLER ANY MORE (2026-09-12). The r2/r3 version read torch.cuda.memory_stats() and
+reset_peak_memory_stats() every VLLM_E2_MEMSTATS seconds. Both take the per-device caching-allocator mutex and the
+Python bindings do not release the GIL, so the old contract's claim that this thread "takes no lock shared with the
+model path" was FALSE. Three one-boot arms at pin 4.1e9 showed a monotone dose-response: sampling at 4 Hz wedged a
+stage-0 worker during A-think-131072; at 1.1 Hz it survived that row and wedged during A-think-260000; with the
+thread disabled the run completed every row. Causation is not established at n=1 per arm (see the Sol review,
+flashnext-e2-capacity-probe-sol-review-2026-09-12), but polling has no place inside a measured region, and it was
+never necessary: PyTorch maintains the allocator high-water marks itself, so reading them once per window is exact.
+
+WHAT THIS THREAD DOES NOW. Between window boundaries it makes NO CUDA calls and touches NO allocator state: its tick
+is a plain read of <dir>/window.ctl, which holds "<seq> <name>". The gate driver crosses a boundary only while the
+server is quiet (no request running). On a new seq the thread performs exactly ONE allocator interaction:
+  1. memory_stats()            -> the closing window's exact high-water marks and cumulative retry/OOM counters
+  2. reset_peak_memory_stats() -> immediately after the read, with no file I/O in the gap
+  3. mem_get_info()            -> device free/total AT THAT QUIESCENT INSTANT
+then writes the "close" record for the old window and the "ack" record for the new one.
+
+WHAT THIS MEASURES.
+  - A row's allocator peak is EXACT for that row's window (not a sampled maximum; it cannot miss a transient).
+  - Device free/total is observed ONLY at quiescent boundaries. No minimum-free figure exists for the inside of a
+    row, and none may be reported.
+  - Retry and OOM counters are cumulative and never reset; per-row deltas come from consecutive close records.
+  - There is NO interval-level peak. The P3F qualifying interval is narrower than its row and is identified only
+    afterwards from the scheduler log, so the peak attributable to P3F is the ROW-level high-water mark, which
+    over-includes the prefill ramp. The prereg states this loss of resolution explicitly.
+
+Records (one JSON per line): kind start | close | ack | error; rank, local, dev, pid; window, seq; and for close:
+alloc_peak_w, res_peak_w, retries, ooms, free, total, run_alloc_peak, run_res_peak. Records are written with
+synchronous file I/O, never inside the read-to-reset gap. Nothing here raises into the worker.
